From: ASRock Rack X570D4I-2T OpenBMC port
Subject: [PATCH] secureCleanup: do not index an empty container
Upstream-Status: Pending

secureCleanup takes the address of element 0 to hand to explicit_bzero. On an
empty container that is out of bounds, and OpenBMC compiles with
-D_GLIBCXX_ASSERTIONS=1, so libstdc++'s hardened operator[] aborts:

  stl_vector.h:1253: ... reference std::vector<...>::operator[](size_type)
  [with _Tp = char]: Assertion '__n < this->size()' failed.

It is reached whenever a mount carries a password that is the empty string:
the password is packed into a std::vector<char>, which is therefore empty, and
SecureBuffer's deleter runs secureCleanup over it. The daemon dies with
SIGABRT partway through InsertMedia -- inside the VolatileFile constructor,
which is why the failure can also surface as nbdkit reporting a missing
password file.

Use data() and return early when there is nothing to scrub. data() is
well-defined on an empty container, and skipping the call avoids passing a
possibly-null pointer to explicit_bzero. Zero bytes of secret still get
zeroed, which is all that was being asked for.

diff --git a/src/utils.hpp b/src/utils.hpp
index 1d4435b..dd36fe4 100644
--- a/src/utils.hpp
+++ b/src/utils.hpp
@@ -26,7 +26,11 @@ constexpr const size_t secretLimit = 1024;
 template <typename T>
 static void secureCleanup(T& value)
 {
-    auto raw = const_cast<typename T::value_type*>(&value[0]);
+    if (value.empty())
+    {
+        return;
+    }
+    auto raw = const_cast<typename T::value_type*>(value.data());
     explicit_bzero(raw, value.size() * sizeof(*raw));
 }
 
